IIUC https://github.com/google/gvisor/pull/12470/commits/80913f9c71a1e72d7999f736377d8bf245f873a1 requires wrapping the atomic pointer fields with a struct and putting special `stateify` tags on them. I think that is also not a very clean interface.

We should probably add native support to stateify for `atomic.Pointer`; such that it should be able to detect `atomic.Pointer` usage and generate code using .Load() and .Store(). I don't know if the AST parser we use is capable to do this. If not, we can add support for new `stateify` tags like:
```
	ptr atomic.Pointer[int] `state: pointer".(int)"`
```

If you intend to cleanup `atomicptr` from go_generics, then we could also just use:
```
// +stateify savable
type S struct {
	ptr atomic.Pointer[int] `state:".(int)"`
}

func (s *S) savePtr() int {
	return s.ptr.Load()
}

func (s *S) loadPtr(_ goContext.Context, ptr int) {
	s.ptr.Store(ptr)
}
```

@tamird This is quite the spam. Merging PRs today is a huge lift because it requires 2 approvals from Googlers and requires constant submit attempts from Googlers because our presubmits are really long and flaky. (I am no longer a Googler and have pending PRs for multiple weeks now)

Are these PRs reviewed by you? They do more than just change checklocks.

So many commits will also pollute our git commit history with checklocks commits. Please squash all your work into 2-3 PRs, probably divided by subsystem. For example, all checklock changes for netstack can go into 1 commit.

`fieldValueAddress(fieldValue)` is invariant across iterations. We can hoist it before the loop to avoid recomputing it in each binary search step:

```go
targetAddr := fieldValueAddress(fieldValue)
low, high := 0, len(field.valuesSorted)
for low < high {
	mid := low + (high-low)/2
	if fieldValueAddress(field.valuesSorted[mid].value) < targetAddr {
		low = mid + 1
	} else {
		high = mid
	}
}
```

`slices.SortedFunc(maps.Values(valueIndices), ...)` collects an iterator into a slice by starting with a nil slice and growing it via repeated `append`s, triggering multiple slice reallocations.

Since `allowedValues` has a known length, we can pre-allocate `valuesSorted` and sort in-place with `slices.SortFunc`:

```go
valuesSorted := make([]fieldValueIndex, len(allowedValues))
for i, v := range allowedValues {
	valuesSorted[i] = fieldValueIndex{value: v, index: i}
}
slices.SortFunc(valuesSorted, func(a, b fieldValueIndex) int {
	return cmp.Compare(fieldValueAddress(a.value), fieldValueAddress(b.value))
})
```

This avoids iterator closures and slice reallocations, and would also allow `valueIndices` above to remain a lightweight `map[string]struct{}`.

AI nit:

#### 1. Trailing Whitespace on //go:nosplit Directive

  In checkescape.go:

    if decl, ok := fn.Syntax().(*ast.FuncDecl); ok && decl.Doc != nil {
        for _, comment := range decl.Doc.List {
            if comment.Text == "//go:nosplit" {
                return nil
            }
        }
    }

  • Observation: comment.Text includes the full comment text including any trailing whitespace or carriage returns (e.g. //go:nosplit  or \r\n). In cmd/compile, the compiler trims
  trailing whitespace when checking compiler directives.
  • Suggestion: Consider strings.TrimSpace(comment.Text) == "//go:nosplit" to guard against accidental trailing whitespace.
